eed  very very clearly put  and let me turn for the third pitch to  mark salter  thank you i think there's something very  revealing about the title of this debate  we're talking about anxiety like it's a  thing in fact like it's a kind of fungus  that must be eradicated at all costs a  disease  but it's more than that it's not a thing  it's a process  and another thing is that the words got  the word the title's got the word  rational  in it there's nothing really that  rational about anxiety in fact it's  important because  anxiety switches off rationality and  that's a very important point  what anxiety is is a process and it's a  mechanism and here's  i think a killer point it is common to  every  living thing on this planet you can just  certain anxiety in the behavior of  bacteria  of a bee the tree even  and i'm not looking about that and what  it does basically  is it is a situation that's been  confirmed upon us by 1.4  billion years of life i.e evolution  to actually be on the constant lookout  for things we talk about  fight or flight mechanisms that's only a  fraction of it  what really happens is that we are  constantly monitoring one part of our  brain  every single thing that is happening in  us inside  and around us outside at the minute  there's a discrepancy  there's an anomaly a mechanism switches  on that says oh  and you homing on it your body will  evaluate it in a nano  second far faster than a human thought  could ever go  and if it's significant it will divert  all the resources of the human brain  towards it  it will switch off every unwanted app  including rational  thought and it will make us take action  and it's not just flight or fight  it's freeze and it's faff now  this mechanism has served us pretty well  for the hundred thousand years that  we've been on this planet which is not  very long  we'd have quite simple lives we hunted  we gathered it was sex and  saber-toothed tigers for the most most  of it but in the last  5 000 years 